## Requirements

| Tool | Needed for | Install |
|---|---|---|
| `mongodump` / `mongorestore` | backup, restore | `sudo apt install mongodb-database-tools` |
| `mongosh` | listing databases and users, interactive pickers | `sudo apt install mongodb-mongosh` |
| `bash` 4+ | everything | preinstalled on Ubuntu |

Backup and restore work without `mongosh`, but the database picker and the
`databases` / `users` commands need it.

<details>
<summary>Adding MongoDB's apt repository (Ubuntu 24.04)</summary>

```bash
sudo apt install -y gnupg curl ca-certificates

curl -fsSL https://www.mongodb.org/static/pgp/server-8.0.asc | \
  sudo gpg -o /usr/share/keyrings/mongodb-server-8.0.gpg --dearmor

echo "deb [ arch=amd64,arm64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/mongodb-server-8.0.gpg ] https://repo.mongodb.org/apt/ubuntu noble/mongodb-org/8.0 multiverse" | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mongodb-org-8.0.list

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y mongodb-database-tools mongodb-mongosh
```

Install **only** these two packages. The `mongodb-org` metapackage would also
install a second `mongod` that fights for port 27017.

</details>

---

## Installation

System-wide, so one copy serves every project:

```bash
sudo install -m 755 mongo-tool.sh /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool
```

Or keep it alongside a single project:

```bash
cp mongo-tool.sh /opt/mongodb/
chmod +x /opt/mongodb/mongo-tool.sh
```

---

## Configuration

Create a `.env` next to your project. Only the password is required — everything
else falls back to sensible defaults.

```bash
MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD="your-password-here"
MONGO_HOST=127.0.0.1
MONGO_PORT=27017
MONGO_USER=admin
MONGO_AUTH_DB=admin
BACKUP_DIR=./backup
RETENTION_DAYS=14
```

Lock it down:

```bash
chmod 600 .env
```

### Recognised keys

| Key | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_PASSWORD` | — | **Required.** `MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD` and `MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD` also accepted |
| `MONGO_HOST` | `127.0.0.1` | Any reachable host |
| `MONGO_PORT` | `27017` | |
| `MONGO_USER` | `admin` | |
| `MONGO_AUTH_DB` | `admin` | Where the user record lives, not the database you're dumping |
| `BACKUP_DIR` | directory holding the `.env` | Relative paths anchor to the `.env`, not your shell's location |
| `RETENTION_DAYS` | `14` | |

### Where `.env` is found

Searched in order, first match wins:

1. `--env /path/to/.env`
2. `$ENV_FILE`
3. `./.env` — your current directory
4. the directory containing the script

This is what lets one installed copy serve many projects: `cd` into the project
and run it.

### Precedence

Highest wins:

```
CLI flag  →  exported env var  →  .env  →  built-in default
```

Check what actually resolved, and where each value came from:

```bash
mongo-tool config
```

```
Resolved configuration
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  env file        /opt/mongodb/.env
  host            127.0.0.1                  .env
  port            27017                      .env
  user            admin                      .env
  authdb          admin                      .env
  backup dir      /opt/mongodb/backup        .env
  retention       14 days                    .env
  password        •••••• (8 chars)           .env (MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  🔧 mongodump  100.18.0
  🔧 mongosh    2.10.0
```

Run this first whenever something misbehaves.

---

## Quick start

```bash
cd /opt/mongodb        # wherever your .env lives

mongo-tool config      # verify settings and tool availability
mongo-tool databases   # confirm you can connect
mongo-tool backup      # pick a database, or Enter for all
```

---

## Commands

### Interactive menu

Run with no arguments:

```bash
mongo-tool
```

```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
  🍃 mongo-tool  admin@127.0.0.1:27017
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
🗄️  INSPECT
   1) List databases
   2) List users
💾 BACKUP & RESTORE
   3) Backup database
   4) Restore database
⚙️  SETTINGS
   5) Show configuration
   0) Exit
```

A failed action reports the error and returns you to the menu rather than exiting.

### `databases`

```bash
mongo-tool databases
```

```
📂 Databases on 127.0.0.1:27017
   admin                        0.10 MB
   config                       0.07 MB
   local                        0.08 MB
   share_chautari              21.61 MB
```

### `users`

```bash
mongo-tool users
```

```
👥 Users  (name@db → roles)
   admin@admin                       root:admin
   appuser@admin                     readWrite:share_chautari
```

### `backup`

```bash
mongo-tool backup                      # interactive picker
mongo-tool backup --all                # entire instance
mongo-tool backup --db share_chautari  # one database
```

Archives are named:

```
mongo-<label>-YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS.archive.gz
```

`--all` produces `mongo-ALL-…` and includes `admin`, so your **user accounts and
roles are captured too**. Take one of these before any rebuild.

### `restore`

See [Restoring](#restoring) below.

### `config`

Prints resolved settings and installed tool versions. Never connects to the
database, so it's safe to run anywhere.

---

## Options

| Option | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--all` | Back up every database |
| `--db NAME` | Back up a single database |
| `--env FILE` | Use a specific `.env` |
| `--host H` | Override host |
| `--port N` | Override port |
| `--user U` | Override username |
| `--out DIR` | Override output directory |
| `--quiet`, `-q` | Suppress normal output; still reports failures |
| `--force` | Skip the restore confirmation prompt |
| `--drop` | Restore drops collections first (replace instead of merge) |
| `--help`, `-h` | Usage summary |

---

## Cron / automation

The silent one-liner:

```cron
0 */6 * * * cd /opt/mongodb && /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool backup --all --quiet
```

Every six hours. `--quiet` prints nothing on success, so cron only emails you when
something fails.

The `cd` matters — it determines which `.env` is picked up and where archives land.

Other schedules:

```cron
# Every 4 hours
0 */4 * * * cd /opt/mongodb && /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool backup --all --quiet

# Daily at 02:30
30 2 * * * cd /opt/mongodb && /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool backup --all --quiet

# One database only
30 2 * * * cd /opt/mongodb && /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool backup --db share_chautari --quiet
```

To keep a log instead of mail:

```cron
0 */6 * * * cd /opt/mongodb && /usr/local/bin/mongo-tool backup --all >> /var/log/mongo-backup.log 2>&1
```

When stdin isn't a terminal, the script skips all prompts and backs up everything —
so a bare `mongo-tool` in cron does the right thing even without `--all`.

### 🔺 Copy backups off the host

A backup on the same disk as the database survives an accidental drop, but not a
disk failure. Add this after the backup:

```cron
15 */6 * * * rsync -az /opt/mongodb/backup/ user@backup-host:/backups/mongo/
```

---

## Restoring

Interactive — lists available archives newest first:

```bash
mongo-tool restore
```

```
🗂️  Available backups (newest first)
  [0] mongo-ALL-2026-08-16-1030.archive.gz          4.2M
  [1] mongo-ALL-2026-08-15-0430.archive.gz          4.1M

📌 Select backup to restore [0-1]:
```

Direct:

```bash
mongo-tool restore backup/mongo-ALL-2026-08-16-1030.archive.gz
```

### Merge vs replace

| Mode | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| default | **Merge** — existing documents are kept, archive contents added |
| `--drop` | **Replace** — each collection is emptied before reload |

Use `--drop` when recovering from corruption or rolling back. Use the default when
re-adding data to a live database.

```bash
mongo-tool restore backup/mongo-ALL-2026-08-16-1030.archive.gz --drop
```

You must type `yes` to confirm. `--force` skips that prompt — needed for scripted
restores, and worth thinking twice about.

Without `--force`, a non-interactive restore is **refused outright**, so a stray cron
entry cannot silently overwrite production.

### Verify an archive without writing anything

```bash
mongorestore --host 127.0.0.1 --port 27017 \
  -u admin -p 'PASSWORD' --authenticationDatabase admin \
  --archive=backup/mongo-ALL-2026-08-16-1030.archive.gz --gzip --dry-run
```

Lists collections and document counts. An untested backup isn't a backup.

---

## Retention

After each successful backup, archives older than `RETENTION_DAYS` are deleted.

Cleanup only ever touches files matching `mongo-*.archive.gz` in the top level of
`BACKUP_DIR` — nothing else in that directory is at risk, which matters because
archives often sit beside `docker-compose.yml` and `.env`.

```bash
RETENTION_DAYS=30 mongo-tool backup --all    # one-off override
```

---

## How it stays safe

**The password never appears in `ps`.** `mongodump` has no `--password-file`, and
`-p secret` is visible to every user on the machine for the duration of the dump.
The script writes a `0600` temporary file and passes `--config`, removing it on exit
via a trap.

**`.env` is parsed, not sourced.** `source .env` executes the file — a stray
backtick would run as a command. The script extracts values with `grep`, handling
quotes, spaces, and `export` prefixes.

**Truncated archives are treated as failures.** A dump that dies early still leaves
a header-only file. Anything under 1 KB is deleted and reported as failed, so a
broken backup can't sit there looking successful.

**Relative paths anchor to the `.env`.** `BACKUP_DIR=./backup` always means the
project's backup directory, never wherever cron happened to start.

**Restore is guarded.** Confirmation required, merge by default, and refused
non-interactively without `--force`.

---

## Troubleshooting

Start here every time:

```bash
mongo-tool config
```

### `mongodump not found`

```bash
sudo apt install mongodb-database-tools
```

### `This action needs mongosh`

Listing and the pickers need it; backup and restore don't.

```bash
sudo apt install mongodb-mongosh
```

### `No password found`

`mongo-tool config` shows which `.env` was located, if any. Either you're in the
wrong directory, or the file uses a key name that isn't recognised. Use
`--env /full/path/.env` to be explicit.

### `mongosh could not connect`

The real error from `mongosh` is printed beneath this line. Common causes:

- Wrong port — check against `sudo ss -tlnp | grep 27017`
- Wrong `MONGO_AUTH_DB`; it's usually `admin` even when dumping another database
- Container not running — `docker ps`

### `Archive only N bytes — treating as failed`

The dump failed after writing headers. Authentication is the usual cause. Run
`mongo-tool databases` to test the connection on its own.

### Backups land in an unexpected directory

`mongo-tool config` prints the resolved absolute `backup dir`. Relative values
resolve against the `.env` location, so this is nearly always a case of a different
`.env` being found than you expected.

### A failed run with no explanation

Every failure prints a call stack:

```
❌ Failed (exit 1) 💥
❌    ↳ cmd_backup() at line 218
❌    ↳ main() at line 475
```

Read top to bottom — the first line is where it actually broke.

---

## Reference

```
mongo-tool                          interactive menu
mongo-tool backup                   pick a database
mongo-tool backup --all             every database
mongo-tool backup --db NAME         one database
mongo-tool backup --all --quiet     silent, for cron
mongo-tool restore                  pick an archive
mongo-tool restore FILE --drop      replace collections
mongo-tool databases                list databases with sizes
mongo-tool users                    list users with roles
mongo-tool config                   show resolved settings
mongo-tool --help                   usage
```